99,150,99,149,99,146,98,145,98,144,96,142,95,142,92,139,92,138,90,137,90,136,89,135,89,135,89,133,99,134\" alt=\"2026 United States Senate election in Texas\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Texas\" id=\"mwPw\"/><area href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Virginia\" shape=\"poly\" coords=\"220,100,223,98,223,97,226,94,228,95,229,95,230,95,231,95,232,95,233,93,233,94,234,93,236,92,236,90,237,88,238,86,238,84,239,85,240,85,240,84,240,82,242,82,242,81,243,80,243,80,244,79,244,77,247,79,247,78,249,80,249,82,250,81,250,83,251,83,253,84,254,84,255,85,256,85,256,85,256,87,257,87,257,88,257,88,256,88,256,89,257,89,257,90,257,90,257,91,257,92,258,92,259,91,259,90,259,88,259,87,259,85,260,84,261,83,261,84,260,85,260,89,260,90,259,89,259,91,260,93,241,97,228,99,228,99\" alt=\"2026 United States Senate election in Virginia\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Virginia\" id=\"mwQA\"/><area href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_West_Virginia\" shape=\"poly\" coords=\"232,76,231,71,230,71,230,77,229,79,228,80,227,80,226,81,226,82,226,82,226,83,225,83,225,83,224,84,224,86,224,86,224,87,222,87,222,89,223,90,224,90,224,91,226,93,226,93,227,95,229,95,230,94,231,94,232,94,232,92,233,93,234,92,235,92,235,91,235,91,235,90,237,86,237,84,238,84,239,84,240,84,240,82,241,81,241,81,243,80,243,79,243,77,244,77,246,78,246,77,246,76,245,75,244,75,243,76,241,76,240,78,240,78,238,80,237,80,237,76\" alt=\"2026 United States Senate election in West Virginia\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in West Virginia\" id=\"mwQQ\"/><area href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Wyoming\" shape=\"poly\" coords=\"73,68,78,41,93,43,111,45,110,61,108,72\" alt=\"2026 United States Senate election in Wyoming\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Wyoming\" id=\"mwQg\"/></map><figcaption id=\"mwQw\"></figcaption></figure><div><b>Map of the incumbents:</b><br/><span style=\"margin:0px; font-size:90%;\"><span style=\"border:none; background-color:#92C5DE; color:#92C5DE;\"><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span>Democratic incumbent</span> <span style=\"margin:0px; font-size:90%;\"><span style=\"border:none; background-color:#0671B0; color:#0671B0;\"><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span>Democratic incumbent retiring</span><br/><span style=\"margin:0px; font-size:90%;\"><span style=\"border:none; background-color:#F48882; color:#F48882;\"><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span>Republican incumbent</span> <span style=\"margin:0px; font-size:90%;\"><span style=\"border:none; background-color:#CA0020; color:#CA0020;\"><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span>Republican incumbent retiring or lost renomination</span><sup about=\"#mwt19\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-OK_4-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"OK\",\"group\":\"lower-alpha\"},\"body\":{\"html\":\"\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-OK-4\" data-mw-group=\"lower-alpha\" id=\"mwRA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwRQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwRg\">[</span>d<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwRw\">]</span></span></a></sup> <br/><span style=\"margin:0px; font-size:90%;\"><span style=\"border:none; background-color:#D3D3D3; color:#D3D3D3;\"><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span>No election</span></div></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"infobox-full-data\"><hr/>\n<table role=\"presentation\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"background:transparent; color: inherit; width:100%;\">\n<tbody><tr><td style=\"width:50%; text-align:left;\"><b>Incumbent <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majority_Leader_of_the_United_States_Senate\" title=\"Majority Leader of the United States Senate\" class=\"mw-redirect\">majority leader</a></b><br/>\n<p><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Thune\" title=\"John Thune\">John Thune</a><br/>\n<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_(United_States)\" title=\"Republican Party (United States)\">Republican</a></p></td>\n<td style=\"width:50%; text-align:right;\"><br/>\n<p>\n<br/></p></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<p id=\"mwSA\">The <b id=\"mwSQ\">2026 United States Senate elections</b> are scheduled to be held on November 3, 2026, with 33 of the 100 seats in the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Senate\" title=\"United States Senate\" id=\"mwSg\">Senate</a> being contested in regular elections. In these elections, voters will elect candidates to six-year terms that begin on January 3, 2027, and expire on January 3, 2033.</p>\n\n<p id=\"mwSw\">Senators are divided into three <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classes_of_United_States_senators\" title=\"Classes of United States senators\" id=\"mwTA\">classes</a> whose terms are staggered, so that a different class is elected every two years. Class 2 senators were last elected in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_Senate_elections\" title=\"2020 United States Senate elections\" id=\"mwTQ\">2020</a> and are up for election in 2026. With the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Senate_Republican_Conference_leadership_election\" title=\"2024 Senate Republican Conference leadership election\" id=\"mwTg\">election</a> of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Thune\" title=\"John Thune\" id=\"mwTw\">John Thune</a> as leader of the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senate_Republican_Conference\" title=\"Senate Republican Conference\" id=\"mwUA\">Republican Conference</a>, this will be the first election year since <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_United_States_Senate_elections\" title=\"2006 United States Senate elections\" id=\"mwUQ\">2006</a> in which Senate Republicans are not led by <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitch_McConnell\" title=\"Mitch McConnell\" id=\"mwUg\">Mitch McConnell</a>. The <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senate_Democratic_Caucus\" title=\"Senate Democratic Caucus\" id=\"mwUw\">Senate Democratic Conference</a> has been led by <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Schumer\" title=\"Chuck Schumer\" id=\"mwVA\">Chuck Schumer</a> since 2017.</p>\n\n<p id=\"mwVQ\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_elections\" title=\"Special elections\" class=\"mw-redirect\" id=\"mwVg\">Special elections</a> have been scheduled in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ohio\" title=\"Ohio\" id=\"mwVw\">Ohio</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florida\" title=\"Florida\" id=\"mwWA\">Florida</a>, following the resignation of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JD_Vance\" title=\"JD Vance\" id=\"mwWQ\">JD Vance</a> to become <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice_President_of_the_United_States\" title=\"Vice President of the United States\" id=\"mwWg\">vice president</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marco_Rubio\" title=\"Marco Rubio\" id=\"mwWw\">Marco Rubio</a>'s resignation to become <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_secretary_of_state\" title=\"United States secretary of state\" class=\"mw-redirect\" id=\"mwXA\">United States secretary of state</a>.</p>\n\n<p id=\"mwXQ\">Two incumbent senators—<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Cassidy\" title=\"Bill Cassidy\" id=\"mwXg\">Bill Cassidy</a> of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louisiana\" title=\"Louisiana\" id=\"mwXw\">Louisiana</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Cornyn\" title=\"John Cornyn\" id=\"mwYA\">John Cornyn</a> of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas\" title=\"Texas\" id=\"mwYQ\">Texas</a>—lost renomination in their respective primaries. The last elected incumbent senator to lose renomination was <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Lugar\" title=\"Richard Lugar\" id=\"mwYg\">Richard Lugar</a> in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_United_States_Senate_election_in_Indiana\" title=\"2012 United States Senate election in Indiana\" id=\"mwYw\">2012</a>,<sup about=\"#mwt21\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-PrimaryLoss_5-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"PrimaryLoss\",\"group\":\"lower-alpha\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-PrimaryLoss-5\"},\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"efn\",\"href\":\"./Template:Efn\"},\"params\":{\"name\":{\"wt\":\"PrimaryLoss\"},\"1\":{\"wt\":\"Republican senator [[Luther Strange]] ran to complete the term he was appointed to in [[2017 United States Senate special election in Alabama|2017]] but lost the primary to [[Roy Moore]], who narrowly lost the general election to Democratic nominee [[Doug Jones (politician)|Doug Jones]].\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'><a href=\"#cite_note-PrimaryLoss-5\" data-mw-group=\"lower-alpha\" id=\"mwZA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwZQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwZg\">[</span>e<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwZw\">]</span></span></a></sup> and the last time multiple elected incumbent senators lost renomination in primaries was in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_United_States_Senate_elections\" title=\"2010 United States Senate elections\" id=\"mwaA\">2010</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt24\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-6\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"group\":\"lower-alpha\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6\"},\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"efn\",\"href\":\"./Template:Efn\"},\"params\":{\"1\":{\"wt\":\"Two elected incumbent senators lost renomination in primaries in 2010: [[Lisa Murkowski]] in [[2010 United States Senate election in Alaska|Alaska]] and [[Arlen Specter]] in [[2010 United States Senate Democratic primary election in Pennsylvania|Pennsylvania]]. After losing her primary, Murkowski ran a write-in campaign during the general election and won. In addition, [[Bob Bennett (politician)|Bob Bennett]] lost renomination at the state convention in [[2010 United States Senate election in Utah|Utah]].\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'><a href=\"#cite_note-6\" data-mw-group=\"lower-alpha\" id=\"mwaQ\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwag\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwaw\">[</span>f<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwbA\">]</span></span></a></sup> The last incumbent senator to place no better than third in a primary before Cassidy was <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hattie_Caraway\" title=\"Hattie Caraway\" id=\"mwbQ\">Hattie Caraway</a> in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1944_United_States_Senate_election_in_Arkansas\" title=\"1944 United States Senate election in Arkansas\" id=\"mwbg\">1944</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt35\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-SittingSenatorsPlaceThird_7-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"SittingSenatorsPlaceThird\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-SittingSenatorsPlaceThird-7\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-SittingSenatorsPlaceThird-7\" id=\"mwbw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwcA\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwcQ\">[</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwcg\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt27\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-8\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"group\":\"lower-alpha\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8\"},\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"efn\",\"href\":\"./Template:Efn\"},\"params\":{\"1\":{\"wt\":\"Republican senator [[Maurice J. Murphy Jr.]] ran to complete the term he was appointed to in [[1962 United States Senate special election in New Hampshire|1962]] but placed third in the primary.&lt;ref name=\\\"SittingSenatorsPlaceThird\\\" />\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'><a href=\"#cite_note-8\" data-mw-group=\"lower-alpha\" id=\"mwcw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwdA\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwdQ\">[</span>g<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwdg\">]</span></span></a></sup></p> \n\n<p id=\"mwdw\">Two nominees—<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_Platner\" title=\"Graham Platner\" id=\"mweA\">Graham Platner</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindsey_Graham\" title=\"Lindsey Graham\" id=\"mweQ\">Lindsey Graham</a>—were replaced, respectively by a convention and a special primary, as their party's nominee in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Maine\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Maine\" id=\"mweg\">Maine</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_South_Carolina\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina\" id=\"mwew\">South Carolina</a>. The last nominee to be replaced by a convention or special primary was <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Walsh_(Montana_politician)\" title=\"John Walsh (Montana politician)\" id=\"mwfA\">John Walsh</a> in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_United_States_Senate_election_in_Montana\" title=\"2014 United States Senate election in Montana\" id=\"mwfQ\">2014</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt36\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-9\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"group\":\"lower-alpha\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9\"},\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"efn\",\"href\":\"./Template:Efn\"},\"params\":{\"1\":{\"wt\":\"Two nominees in subsequent election cycles willingly withdrew their nominations after winning their primaries: [[Baron Hill (politician)|Baron Hill]] in the [[2016 United States Senate election in Indiana|2016 election in Indiana]] and [[H. Brooke Paige]] in the [[2018 United States Senate election in Vermont|2018 election in Vermont]]. The replacement nominees were selected by their parties without a convention or special primary; both lost in the general election.\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'><a href=\"#cite_note-9\" data-mw-group=\"lower-alpha\" id=\"mwfg\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwfw\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwgA\">[</span>h<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwgQ\">]</span></span></a></sup> The last time multiple nominees were replaced was in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2002_United_States_Senate_elections\" title=\"2002 United States Senate elections\" id=\"mwgg\">2002</a>, with <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Torricelli\" title=\"Robert Torricelli\" id=\"mwgw\">Robert Torricelli</a> of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Jersey\" title=\"New Jersey\" id=\"mwhA\">New Jersey</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Wellstone\" title=\"Paul Wellstone\" id=\"mwhQ\">Paul Wellstone</a> of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnesota\" title=\"Minnesota\" id=\"mwhg\">Minnesota</a>.</p>\n\n<p id=\"mwhw\">In three states (<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idaho\" title=\"Idaho\" id=\"mwiA\">Idaho</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nebraska\" title=\"Nebraska\" id=\"mwiQ\">Nebraska</a>, and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Dakota\" title=\"South Dakota\" id=\"mwig\">South Dakota</a>), the Democratic nominee has withdrawn from the race, effectively clearing the field for an independent.<sup about=\"#mwt41\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-10\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-10\" id=\"mwiw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwjA\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwjQ\">[</span>2<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwjg\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<meta property=\"mw:PageProp/toc\" id=\"mwjw\" data-mw='{\"autoGenerated\":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id=\"1\" id=\"mwkA\" aria-labelledby=\"Partisan_composition\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"Partisan_composition\">Partisan composition</h2><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Partisan composition\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<p id=\"mwkQ\">All 33 <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classes_of_United_States_senators\" title=\"Classes of United States senators\" id=\"mwkg\">Class 2</a> Senate seats and two <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classes_of_United_States_senators\" title=\"Classes of United States senators\" id=\"mwkw\">Class 3</a> seats are up for election in 2026. Class 2 currently consists of 20 Republicans and 13 Democrats.<sup about=\"#mwt44\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-11\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-11\" id=\"mwlA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwlQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwlg\">[</span>3<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwlw\">]</span></span></a></sup> Democrats are defending two seats in states won by <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump\" title=\"Donald Trump\" id=\"mwmA\">Donald Trump</a> in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election\" title=\"2024 United States presidential election\" id=\"mwmQ\">2024</a>, in both cases by less than 3 percentage points: <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Michigan\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Michigan\" id=\"mwmg\">Michigan</a>, where incumbent <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Peters\" title=\"Gary Peters\" id=\"mwmw\">Gary Peters</a> is not running for reelection; and Georgia, where the incumbent is <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jon_Ossoff\" title=\"Jon Ossoff\" id=\"mwnA\">Jon Ossoff</a>. Michigan elected Democrat <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elissa_Slotkin\" title=\"Elissa Slotkin\" id=\"mwnQ\">Elissa Slotkin</a> to the Senate in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_Senate_election_in_Michigan\" title=\"2024 United States Senate election in Michigan\" id=\"mwng\">2024</a> by 0.3 percentage points. Georgia did not have a Senate election in 2024, although in the most recent Senate election, in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_United_States_Senate_election_in_Georgia\" title=\"2022 United States Senate election in Georgia\" id=\"mwnw\">2022</a>, Democrat <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raphael_Warnock\" title=\"Raphael Warnock\" id=\"mwoA\">Raphael Warnock</a> won by three points in a runoff.<sup about=\"#mwt47\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-margin_12-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"margin\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-margin-12\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-margin-12\" id=\"mwoQ\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwog\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwow\">[</span>4<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwpA\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt50\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-13\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-13\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-13\" id=\"mwpQ\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwpg\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwpw\">[</span>5<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwqA\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<p id=\"mwqQ\">Five incumbent Democratic senators represent states won by <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamala_Harris\" title=\"Kamala Harris\" id=\"mwqg\">Kamala Harris</a> by single-digit margins in 2024: New Hampshire's <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeanne_Shaheen\" title=\"Jeanne Shaheen\" id=\"mwqw\">Jeanne Shaheen</a>, Minnesota's <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tina_Smith\" title=\"Tina Smith\" id=\"mwrA\">Tina Smith</a>, Virginia's <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Warner\" title=\"Mark Warner\" id=\"mwrQ\">Mark Warner</a>, New Jersey's <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cory_Booker\" title=\"Cory Booker\" id=\"mwrg\">Cory Booker</a>, and New Mexico's <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Ray_Luján\" title=\"Ben Ray Luján\" id=\"mwrw\">Ben Ray Luján</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt51\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-margin_12-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"margin\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-margin-12\" id=\"mwsA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwsQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwsg\">[</span>4<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwsw\">]</span></span></a></sup> Shaheen and Smith are not running for reelection.<sup about=\"#mwt54\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-14\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-14\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-14\" id=\"mwtA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwtQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwtg\">[</span>6<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwtw\">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susan_Collins\" title=\"Susan Collins\" id=\"mwuA\">Susan Collins</a>'s seat in Maine is the only seat being defended by a Republican in a state that Kamala Harris won in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election_in_Maine\" title=\"2024 United States presidential election in Maine\" id=\"mwuQ\">2024</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt57\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-15\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-15\" id=\"mwug\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwuw\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwvA\">[</span>7<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwvQ\">]</span></span></a></sup> Republican <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thom_Tillis\" title=\"Thom Tillis\" id=\"mwvg\">Thom Tillis</a> of North Carolina is retiring<sup about=\"#mwt60\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-politico_carney_16-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"politico carney\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-politico_carney-16\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-politico_carney-16\" id=\"mwvw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwwA\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwwQ\">[</span>8<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwwg\">]</span></span></a></sup> from the Senate in a state that Trump won by a single-digit margin in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election_in_North_Carolina\" title=\"2024 United States presidential election in North Carolina\" id=\"mwww\">2024</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt61\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-margin_12-2\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"margin\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-margin-12\" id=\"mwxA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwxQ\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwxg\">[</span>4<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwxw\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<p id=\"mwyA\">The 2026 Senate map is considered favorable to Republicans, even though Democrats are defending 13 seats and Republicans 22. Most rating groups identify two Republican-held seats as highly competitive and three or four others as somewhat competitive. By contrast, Democrats must flip at least four seats to win a majority while defending two highly vulnerable seats and two others viewed as somewhat vulnerable.<sup about=\"#mwt64\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-17\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-17\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-17\" id=\"mwyQ\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwyg\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwyw\">[</span>9<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwzA\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt67\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-18\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-18\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-18\" id=\"mwzQ\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwzg\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwzw\">[</span>10<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw0A\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt70\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-19\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-19\" id=\"mw0Q\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mw0g\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw0w\">[</span>11<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw1A\">]</span></span></a></sup> Pollsters have found Democrats' chances to flip the Senate to be improving due to nominees in states such as <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_North_Carolina\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in North Carolina\" id=\"mw1Q\">North Carolina</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_special_election_in_Ohio\" title=\"2026 United States Senate special election in Ohio\" id=\"mw1g\">Ohio</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Texas\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Texas\" id=\"mw1w\">Texas</a>, and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Alaska\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Alaska\" id=\"mw2A\">Alaska</a> running highly competitive campaigns, and because of <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump\" title=\"Donald Trump\" id=\"mw2Q\">Donald Trump</a>'s unpopularity amid the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_war\" title=\"2026 Iran war\" id=\"mw2g\">2026 Iran war</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt73\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-20\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-20\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-20\" id=\"mw2w\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mw3A\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw3Q\">[</span>12<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw3g\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt76\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-21\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-21\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-21\" id=\"mw3w\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mw4A\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw4Q\">[</span>13<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mw4g\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"2\" id=\"mw4w\" aria-labelledby=\"Summary_results\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"Summary_results\">Summary results</h2><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Summary results\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<section data-mw-section-id=\"3\" id=\"mw5A\" aria-labelledby=\"Seats\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\"><h3 id=\"Seats\">Seats</h3><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit se