+8.0%</b></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RealClearPolitics\" title=\"RealClearPolitics\">RealClearPolitics</a><sup about=\"#mwt1937\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-65\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-65\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-65\" id=\"mwCeY\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCec\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCeg\">[</span>42<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCek\">]</span></span></a></sup></td>\n<td>September 21 – October 13, 2026</td>\n<td>October 14, 2026</td>\n<td>42.3%</td>\n<td style=\"color:black;background-color:#B0CEFF\" about=\"#mwt1951\" typeof=\"mw:ExpandedAttrs\" data-mw='{\"attribs\":[[{\"txt\":\"style\"},{\"html\":\"color:black;background-color:&lt;span typeof=\\\"mw:Nowiki\\\" id=\\\"mwCeo\\\">&lt;/span>#B0CEFF\"}]]}'><b>50.4%</b></td>\n<td>7.3%</td>\n<td style=\"color:black;background-color:#B0CEFF\" about=\"#mwt1952\" typeof=\"mw:ExpandedAttrs\" data-mw='{\"attribs\":[[{\"txt\":\"style\"},{\"html\":\"color:black;background-color:&lt;span typeof=\\\"mw:Nowiki\\\" id=\\\"mwCes\\\">&lt;/span>#B0CEFF\"}]]}'><b>Democrats +8.1%</b></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nate_Silver\" title=\"Nate Silver\">Silver Bulletin</a><sup about=\"#mwt1942\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-66\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-66\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-66\" id=\"mwCew\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCe0\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCe4\">[</span>43<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCe8\">]</span></span></a></sup></td>\n<td>January 9, 2025 – October 13, 2026</td>\n<td>October 14, 2026</td>\n<td>41.2%</td>\n<td style=\"color:black;background-color:#B0CEFF\" about=\"#mwt1953\" typeof=\"mw:ExpandedAttrs\" data-mw='{\"attribs\":[[{\"txt\":\"style\"},{\"html\":\"color:black;background-color:&lt;span typeof=\\\"mw:Nowiki\\\" id=\\\"mwCfA\\\">&lt;/span>#B0CEFF\"}]]}'><b>49.8%</b></td>\n<td>9.0%</td>\n<td style=\"color:black;background-color:#B0CEFF\" about=\"#mwt1954\" typeof=\"mw:ExpandedAttrs\" data-mw='{\"attribs\":[[{\"txt\":\"style\"},{\"html\":\"color:black;background-color:&lt;span typeof=\\\"mw:Nowiki\\\" id=\\\"mwCfE\\\">&lt;/span>#B0CEFF\"}]]}'><b>Democrats +8.6%</b></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\"><b>Average</b></td>\n<td>October 14, 2026</td>\n<td>42.2%</td>\n<td style=\"color:black;background-color:#B0CEFF\" about=\"#mwt1955\" typeof=\"mw:ExpandedAttrs\" data-mw='{\"attribs\":[[{\"txt\":\"style\"},{\"html\":\"color:black;background-color:&lt;span typeof=\\\"mw:Nowiki\\\" id=\\\"mwCfI\\\">&lt;/span>#B0CEFF\"}]]}'><b>50.4%</b></td>\n<td>7.4%</td>\n<td style=\"color:black;background-color:#B0CEFF\" about=\"#mwt1956\" typeof=\"mw:ExpandedAttrs\" data-mw='{\"attribs\":[[{\"txt\":\"style\"},{\"html\":\"color:black;background-color:&lt;span typeof=\\\"mw:Nowiki\\\" id=\\\"mwCfM\\\">&lt;/span>#B0CEFF\"}]]}'><b>Democrats +8.2%</b></td></tr>\n\n</tbody></table>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"12\" id=\"mwCfQ\" aria-labelledby=\"Potentially_competitive_seats\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"Potentially_competitive_seats\">Potentially competitive seats</h2><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Potentially competitive seats\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<p id=\"mwCfU\">These are seats that polling and predictions currently have listed as at least somewhat close.</p>\n\n<section data-mw-section-id=\"13\" id=\"mwCfY\" aria-labelledby=\"Republican_incumbents_with_Democratic_challengers\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\"><h3 id=\"Republican_incumbents_with_Democratic_challengers\">Republican incumbents with Democratic challengers</h3><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Republican incumbents with Democratic challengers\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<p id=\"mwCfc\">One <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_(United_States)\" title=\"Republican Party (United States)\" id=\"mwCfg\">Republican</a>-held senate seat, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_North_Carolina\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in North Carolina\" id=\"mwCfk\">North Carolina</a>, is considered competitive but favoring the Democrats.<sup about=\"#mwt1957\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-:0_67-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\":0\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-:0-67\" id=\"mwCfo\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCfs\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCfw\">[</span>44<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCf0\">]</span></span></a></sup> Former <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_of_North_Carolina\" title=\"Governor of North Carolina\" id=\"mwCf4\">Governor</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roy_Cooper\" title=\"Roy Cooper\" id=\"mwCf8\">Roy Cooper</a> overwhelmingly won the Democratic nomination for the open seat.<sup about=\"#mwt1960\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-nc_68-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"nc\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-nc-68\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-nc-68\" id=\"mwCgA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCgE\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCgI\">[</span>45<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCgM\">]</span></span></a></sup> Incumbent Republican senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thom_Tillis\" title=\"Thom Tillis\" id=\"mwCgQ\">Thom Tillis</a> is retiring,<sup about=\"#mwt1963\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-Tillis_69-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"Tillis\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tillis-69\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-Tillis-69\" id=\"mwCgU\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCgY\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCgc\">[</span>46<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCgg\">]</span></span></a></sup> with former <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_National_Committee\" title=\"Republican National Committee\" id=\"mwCgk\">Republican National Committee</a> Chairman <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Whatley\" title=\"Michael Whatley\" id=\"mwCgo\">Michael Whatley</a> becoming the Republican nominee, endorsed by <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump\" title=\"Donald Trump\" id=\"mwCgs\">Donald Trump</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt1966\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-70\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-70\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-70\" id=\"mwCgw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCg0\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCg4\">[</span>47<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCg8\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n<p id=\"mwChA\">Six Republican-held Senate races are expected to be highly competitive: <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Maine\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Maine\" id=\"mwChE\">Maine</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_special_election_in_Ohio\" title=\"2026 United States Senate special election in Ohio\" id=\"mwChI\">Ohio</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Alaska\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Alaska\" id=\"mwChM\">Alaska</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Texas\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Texas\" id=\"mwChQ\">Texas</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Iowa\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Iowa\" id=\"mwChU\">Iowa</a>, and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Kansas\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Kansas\" id=\"mwChY\">Kansas</a>. Incumbent <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Susan_Collins\" title=\"Susan Collins\" id=\"mwChc\">Susan Collins</a> is seeking reelection in Maine;<sup about=\"#mwt1969\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-el_71-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"el\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-el-71\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-el-71\" id=\"mwChg\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwChk\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCho\">[</span>48<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwChs\">]</span></span></a></sup> it is regarded as competitive due to the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cook_Partisan_Voting_Index\" title=\"Cook Partisan Voting Index\" id=\"mwChw\">state's blue lean</a>, but Collins kept her seat during cycles favorable to Democrats nationally (<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2008_United_States_Senate_election_in_Maine\" title=\"2008 United States Senate election in Maine\" id=\"mwCh0\">2008</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_Senate_election_in_Maine\" title=\"2020 United States Senate election in Maine\" id=\"mwCh4\">2020</a>). Military veteran <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_Platner\" title=\"Graham Platner\" id=\"mwCh8\">Graham Platner</a> was the Democratic nominee in Maine until he withdrew from the race due to allegations of sexual assault. He was replaced by former <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maine_Senate\" title=\"Maine Senate\" id=\"mwCiA\">Maine Senate</a> president <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Troy_Jackson\" title=\"Troy Jackson\" id=\"mwCiE\">Troy Jackson</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt1972\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-72\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-72\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-72\" id=\"mwCiI\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCiM\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCiQ\">[</span>49<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCiU\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt1975\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-73\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-73\" id=\"mwCiY\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCic\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCig\">[</span>50<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCik\">]</span></span></a></sup> Incumbents in Ohio (<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jon_Husted\" title=\"Jon Husted\" id=\"mwCio\">Jon Husted</a>) and Alaska (<a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dan_Sullivan_(U.S._senator)\" title=\"Dan Sullivan (U.S. senator)\" id=\"mwCis\">Dan Sullivan</a>) are expected to have competitive races against former U.S. senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherrod_Brown\" title=\"Sherrod Brown\" id=\"mwCiw\">Sherrod Brown</a> and former U.S. representative <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Peltola\" title=\"Mary Peltola\" id=\"mwCi0\">Mary Peltola</a>, respectively.<sup about=\"#mwt1976\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-el_71-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"el\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-el-71\" id=\"mwCi4\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCi8\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCjA\">[</span>48<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCjE\">]</span></span></a></sup> Both <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_Senate_election_in_Ohio\" title=\"2024 United States Senate election in Ohio\" id=\"mwCjI\">Brown</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_House_of_Representatives_election_in_Alaska\" title=\"2024 United States House of Representatives election in Alaska\" id=\"mwCjM\">Peltola</a> lost reelection in 2024. In Texas, incumbent Senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Cornyn\" title=\"John Cornyn\" id=\"mwCjQ\">John Cornyn</a> lost the Texas Senate Republican primary election to <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_Attorney_General\" title=\"Texas Attorney General\" id=\"mwCjU\">Texas Attorney General</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Paxton\" title=\"Ken Paxton\" id=\"mwCjY\">Ken Paxton</a>, who is expected to have a competitive race against <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_House_of_Representatives\" title=\"Texas House of Representatives\" id=\"mwCjc\">State Representative</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Talarico\" title=\"James Talarico\" id=\"mwCjg\">James Talarico</a>. In Iowa, the incumbent <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joni_Ernst\" title=\"Joni Ernst\" id=\"mwCjk\">Joni Ernst</a> is not seeking reelection;<sup about=\"#mwt1977\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-ernst_30-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"ernst\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-ernst-30\" id=\"mwCjo\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCjs\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCjw\">[</span>18<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCj0\">]</span></span></a></sup> the race to succeed Ernst between Republican U.S. representative <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashley_Hinson\" title=\"Ashley Hinson\" id=\"mwCj4\">Ashley Hinson</a> and Democratic state representative <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Turek\" title=\"Josh Turek\" id=\"mwCj8\">Josh Turek</a> is expected to be competitive. In Kansas, the gap between the Democratic nominee, pastor <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam_Hamilton_(pastor)\" title=\"Adam Hamilton (pastor)\" id=\"mwCkA\">Adam Hamilton</a>, and incumbent Senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Marshall\" title=\"Roger Marshall\" id=\"mwCkE\">Roger Marshall</a> narrowed in early October polls.   </p>\n\n<p id=\"mwCkI\">Four other Republican-held Senate races are expected to favor Republicans over Democrats but are viewed as somewhat competitive by some predictors: <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_special_election_in_Florida\" title=\"2026 United States Senate special election in Florida\" id=\"mwCkM\">Florida</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Mississippi\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Mississippi\" id=\"mwCkQ\">Mississippi</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_South_Carolina\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina\" id=\"mwCkU\">South Carolina</a>, and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Louisiana\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Louisiana\" id=\"mwCkY\">Louisiana</a>. In Louisiana, incumbent Senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Cassidy\" title=\"Bill Cassidy\" id=\"mwCkc\">Bill Cassidy</a> was challenged by <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louisiana_State_Treasurer\" title=\"Louisiana State Treasurer\" id=\"mwCkg\">Louisiana State Treasurer</a> <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Fleming_(Louisiana_politician)\" title=\"John Fleming (Louisiana politician)\" id=\"mwCkk\">John Fleming</a> and U.S. representative <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_Letlow\" title=\"Julia Letlow\" id=\"mwCko\">Julia Letlow</a>, with Letlow winning the primary election, marking the first time an elected incumbent senator had lost their primary in any state since <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_United_States_Senate_election_in_Indiana\" title=\"2012 United States Senate election in Indiana\" id=\"mwCks\">2012 in Indiana</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt1978\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-PrimaryLoss_5-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"PrimaryLoss\",\"group\":\"lower-alpha\"},\"body\":{\"html\":\"\"},\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"efn\",\"href\":\"./Template:Efn\"},\"params\":{\"name\":{\"wt\":\"PrimaryLoss\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'><a href=\"#cite_note-PrimaryLoss-5\" data-mw-group=\"lower-alpha\" id=\"mwCkw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCk0\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCk4\">[</span>e<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCk8\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"14\" id=\"mwClA\" aria-labelledby=\"Republican_incumbents_with_independent_challengers\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\"><h3 id=\"Republican_incumbents_with_independent_challengers\">Republican incumbents with independent challengers</h3><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Republican incumbents with independent challengers\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<p id=\"mwClE\">Four Senate races with Republican incumbents have <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_politician\" title=\"Independent politician\" id=\"mwClI\">independent</a> candidates as the main challengers to the Republicans: <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Nebraska\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Nebraska\" id=\"mwClM\">Nebraska</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Idaho\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Idaho\" id=\"mwClQ\">Idaho</a>, <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Montana\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Montana\" id=\"mwClU\">Montana</a>, and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_South_Dakota\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in South Dakota\" id=\"mwClY\">South Dakota</a>. In Nebraska, Idaho, and South Dakota, the Democratic candidates dropped out of the race.<sup about=\"#mwt1983\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-74\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-74\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-74\" id=\"mwClc\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwClg\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwClk\">[</span>51<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwClo\">]</span></span></a></sup> In Nebraska, incumbent Senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pete_Ricketts\" title=\"Pete Ricketts\" id=\"mwCls\">Pete Ricketts</a> is challenged by independent <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dan_Osborn\" title=\"Dan Osborn\" id=\"mwClw\">Dan Osborn</a>, who ran against Republican <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deb_Fischer\" title=\"Deb Fischer\" id=\"mwCl0\">Deb Fischer</a> in the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_Senate_election_in_Nebraska\" title=\"2024 United States Senate election in Nebraska\" id=\"mwCl4\">Class I</a> race in 2024. Osborn was the most successful challenger of a Republican-held seat during the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_elections\" title=\"2024 United States elections\" id=\"mwCl8\">2024 election cycle</a>, losing by only 6.7 points, compared to <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamala_Harris\" title=\"Kamala Harris\" id=\"mwCmA\">Kamala Harris</a>'s loss by 20.5 points in Nebraska in the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election_in_Nebraska\" title=\"2024 United States presidential election in Nebraska\" id=\"mwCmE\">concurrent presidential election</a>. In August 2026, the Nebraska Democratic Party endorsed Osborn.<sup about=\"#mwt1986\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-75\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-75\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-75\" id=\"mwCmI\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCmM\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCmQ\">[</span>52<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCmU\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n</section><section data-mw-section-id=\"15\" id=\"mwCmY\" aria-labelledby=\"Democratic_incumbents\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading3\"><h3 id=\"Democratic_incumbents\">Democratic incumbents</h3><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Edit section: Democratic incumbents\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<p id=\"mwCmc\">The <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_(United_States)\" title=\"Democratic Party (United States)\" id=\"mwCmg\">Democratic</a>-held Senate race expected to be the most competitive is in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Michigan\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Michigan\" id=\"mwCmk\">Michigan</a>, where senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Peters\" title=\"Gary Peters\" id=\"mwCmo\">Gary Peters</a> has announced his retirement. The state has an even <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cook_Partisan_Voting_Index\" title=\"Cook Partisan Voting Index\" id=\"mwCms\">PVI</a> score.<sup about=\"#mwt1989\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-76\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-76\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-76\" id=\"mwCmw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCm0\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCm4\">[</span>53<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCm8\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt1990\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-el_71-2\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"el\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-el-71\" id=\"mwCnA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCnE\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCnI\">[</span>48<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCnM\">]</span></span></a></sup> Former US Representative for the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michigan's_8th_congressional_district\" title=\"Michigan's 8th congressional district\" id=\"mwCnQ\">8th congressional district</a> and <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_Senate_election_in_Michigan\" title=\"2024 United States Senate election in Michigan\" id=\"mwCnU\">2024</a> U.S. Senate nominee <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Rogers_(Michigan_politician)\" title=\"Mike Rogers (Michigan politician)\" id=\"mwCnY\">Mike Rogers</a><sup about=\"#mwt1991\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-MRogers_77-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"MRogers\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-MRogers-77\" id=\"mwCnc\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCng\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCnk\">[</span>54<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCno\">]</span></span></a></sup> is the Republican nominee. Former <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayne_County,_Michigan\" title=\"Wayne County, Michigan\" id=\"mwCns\">Wayne County</a> Health, Human, and Veterans Services Department Director <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdul_El-Sayed\" title=\"Abdul El-Sayed\" id=\"mwCnw\">Abdul El-Sayed</a><sup about=\"#mwt1992\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-El_Sayed_78-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"El Sayed\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-El_Sayed-78\" id=\"mwCn0\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCn4\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCn8\">[</span>55<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCoA\">]</span></span></a></sup> is the Democratic nominee. In <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_New_Hampshire\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in New Hampshire\" id=\"mwCoE\">New Hampshire</a>, where incumbent Democratic senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeanne_Shaheen\" title=\"Jeanne Shaheen\" id=\"mwCoI\">Jeanne Shaheen</a> is retiring, the race is also expected to be competitive. U.S. Representative <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chris_Pappas_(American_politician)\" title=\"Chris Pappas (American politician)\" id=\"mwCoM\">Chris Pappas</a> is the Democratic nominee and former U.S. Senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_E._Sununu\" title=\"John E. Sununu\" id=\"mwCoQ\">John E. Sununu</a> is the Republican nominee. Sununu lost to Shaheen in <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2008_United_States_Senate_election_in_New_Hampshire\" title=\"2008 United States Senate election in New Hampshire\" id=\"mwCoU\">2008</a>.<sup about=\"#mwt1993\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-JohnSununu_79-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"JohnSununu\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-JohnSununu-79\" id=\"mwCoY\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCoc\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCog\">[</span>56<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCok\">]</span></span></a></sup> Some polls have shown the race as a dead heat,<sup about=\"#mwt1996\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-80\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-80\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-80\" id=\"mwCoo\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCos\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCow\">[</span>57<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCo0\">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about=\"#mwt1999\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-EmersonNHMarch2026_81-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"EmersonNHMarch2026\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-EmersonNHMarch2026-81\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-EmersonNHMarch2026-81\" id=\"mwCo4\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCo8\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCpA\">[</span>58<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCpE\">]</span></span></a></sup> with two August polls showing Sununu ahead.</p>\n\n<p id=\"mwCpI\">In <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Georgia\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Georgia\" id=\"mwCpM\">Georgia</a>, incumbent Democratic senator <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jon_Ossoff\" title=\"Jon Ossoff\" id=\"mwCpQ\">Jon Ossoff</a> is running against Republican <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Collins_(politician)\" title=\"Mike Collins (politician)\" id=\"mwCpU\">Mike Collins</a>, the <a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._representative\" title=\"U.S. representative\" class=\"mw-redirect\" id=\"mwCpY\">U.S. representative</a> from <span data-sort-value=\"Georgia10 !\" about=\"#mwt2000\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwCpc\" data-mw='{\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"ushr\",\"href\":\"./Template:Ushr\"},\"params\":{\"1\":{\"wt\":\"GA\"},\"2\":{\"wt\":\"10\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia's_10th_congressional_district\" title=\"Georgia's 10th congressional district\">Georgia's 10th congressional district</a></span>.<sup about=\"#mwt2003\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-82\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-82\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-82\" id=\"mwCpg\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCpk\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCpo\">[</span>59<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCps\">]</span></span></a></sup> The race was initially expected to be very competitive,<sup about=\"#mwt2004\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-el_71-3\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"el\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-el-71\" id=\"mwCpw\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCp0\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCp4\">[</span>48<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCp8\">]</span></span></a></sup> but election predictors have become increasingly confident of Ossoff's prospects.<sup about=\"#mwt2007\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-83\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-83\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-83\" id=\"mwCqA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCqE\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCqI\">[</span>60<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCqM\">]</span></span></a></sup></p>\n\n</section></section><section data-mw-section-id=\"16\" id=\"mwCqQ\" aria-labelledby=\"Election_dates\"><div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"Election_dates\">Election dates</h2><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=2026_United_States_Senate_elections&amp;action=edit&amp;section=16\" title=\"Edit section: Election dates\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<table class=\"wikitable sortable\" id=\"mwCqU\">\n<tbody id=\"mwCqY\"><tr id=\"mwCqc\">\n<th id=\"mwCqg\">State</th>\n<th id=\"mwCqk\">Filing deadline for<br id=\"mwCqo\"/>major party candidates<sup about=\"#mwt2010\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-84\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-84\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-84\" id=\"mwCqs\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCqw\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCq0\">[</span>61<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCq4\">]</span></span></a></sup></th>\n<th id=\"mwCq8\">Primary<br id=\"mwCrA\"/>election<sup about=\"#mwt2013\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-sp_85-0\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"sp\"},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-sp-85\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-sp-85\" id=\"mwCrE\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCrI\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCrM\">[</span>62<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCrQ\">]</span></span></a></sup></th>\n<th id=\"mwCrU\">Primary<br id=\"mwCrY\"/>runoff<br id=\"mwCrc\"/><span style=\"font-size: 85%;\" about=\"#mwt2014\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwCrg\" data-mw='{\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"Small\",\"href\":\"./Template:Small\"},\"params\":{\"1\":{\"wt\":\"(if necessary)\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'>(if necessary)</span><sup about=\"#mwt2015\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-sp_85-1\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{\"name\":\"sp\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-sp-85\" id=\"mwCrk\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCro\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCrs\">[</span>62<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCrw\">]</span></span></a></sup></th>\n<th id=\"mwCr0\">General<br id=\"mwCr4\"/>election</th>\n<th id=\"mwCr8\">Poll closing<sup about=\"#mwt2019\" class=\"mw-ref reference\" id=\"cite_ref-86\" rel=\"dc:references\" typeof=\"mw:Extension/ref\" data-mw='{\"name\":\"ref\",\"attrs\":{},\"body\":{\"id\":\"mw-reference-text-cite_note-86\"}}'><a href=\"#cite_note-86\" id=\"mwCsA\"><span class=\"mw-reflink-text\" id=\"mwCsE\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCsI\">[</span>63<span class=\"cite-bracket\" id=\"mwCsM\">]</span></span></a></sup><br id=\"mwCsQ\"/><span style=\"font-size: 85%;\" about=\"#mwt2016\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwCsU\" data-mw='{\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"Small\",\"href\":\"./Template:Small\"},\"params\":{\"1\":{\"wt\":\"(Eastern Time)\"}},\"i\":0}}]}'>(Eastern Time)</span></th></tr>\n<tr id=\"mwCsY\">\n<th id=\"mwCsc\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Alabama\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Alabama\" id=\"mwCsg\">Alabama</a></th>\n<td data-sort-value=\"January 23, 2026\" id=\"mwCsk\">January 23, 2026</td>\n<td data-sort-value=\"May 19, 2026\" id=\"mwCso\">May 19, 2026</td>\n<td data-sort-value=\"June 16, 2026\" id=\"mwCss\">June 16, 2026</td>\n<td rowspan=\"35\" data-sort-value=\"November 3, 2026\" id=\"mwCsw\">November 3, 2026</td>\n<td id=\"mwCs0\">8<span class=\"nowrap\" about=\"#mwt2020\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwCs4\" data-mw='{\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"nbsp\",\"href\":\"./Template:Nbsp\"},\"params\":{},\"i\":0}}]}'><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span>pm</td></tr>\n<tr id=\"mwCs8\">\n<th id=\"mwCtA\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Alaska\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Alaska\" id=\"mwCtE\">Alaska</a></th>\n<td data-sort-value=\"June 1, 2026\" id=\"mwCtI\">June 1, 2026</td>\n<td data-sort-value=\"August 18, 2026\" id=\"mwCtM\">August 18, 2026</td>\n<td data-sort-value=\"November 31, 2026\" id=\"mwCtQ\">N/A</td>\n<td id=\"mwCtU\">12<span class=\"nowrap\" about=\"#mwt2021\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwCtY\" data-mw='{\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"nbsp\",\"href\":\"./Template:Nbsp\"},\"params\":{},\"i\":0}}]}'><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span>am and 1<span class=\"nowrap\" about=\"#mwt2022\" typeof=\"mw:Transclusion\" id=\"mwCtc\" data-mw='{\"parts\":[{\"template\":{\"target\":{\"wt\":\"nbsp\",\"href\":\"./Template:Nbsp\"},\"params\":{},\"i\":0}}]}'><span typeof=\"mw:Entity\"> </span></span>am</td></tr>\n<tr id=\"mwCtg\">\n<th id=\"mwCtk\"><a rel=\"mw:WikiLink\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_election_in_Arkansas\" title=\"2026 United States Senate election in Arkansas\" id=\"mwCto\">Arkansas</a></